## Landing

How `/ship-plan` lands a reviewed plan. `integrate`: `pull-request` pushes the plan's branch and opens a pull request; `direct` pushes to the default branch. `release`: `none`; `tag` tags the plan's `Release:` version; `tag+release` also publishes a release from that version's section of `changelog`. `draft`: `no`; `yes` opens every pull request as a draft, as `/ship-plan draft` does. `follow-ups`: `issues` files a plan's follow-ups as issues in this repository; `<owner/repo>` files them in that repository; `none` files none, and `/ship-plan` lists them for you to record. `follow-up-label`: the label those issues get, `follow-up` by default; write a name with spaces as it is, such as `tech debt`. `protect`: `none`; `hook` makes `.githooks/pre-push` refuse any push to the default branch, for a host that can't require pull requests, such as a private repository on GitHub Free. `/run-issues` runs only when the host requires pull requests into the default branch, or `protect` is `hook` and the hooks are enabled.

- integrate: pull-request
- release: none
- changelog: CHANGELOG.md
- draft: no
- follow-ups: issues
- follow-up-label: follow-up
- protect: none

